# Conventions

Code-quality rules the agent must follow on every task. Auto-loaded each session by `context-loader`. Referenced by `/bencium-next` Phase A (PLAN) and called out if a planned change would violate them.

Keep this file short. If a rule needs a paragraph of justification, link to a decision in `.harness/archive/` instead.

Everything here is a starting default. Change the numbers, delete what doesn't apply, and put your stack's own rules under "Project-specific rules" at the bottom — that section is the point of the file.

## Explain the loop step

At each loop step, before moving on, briefly explain what's being done: what the step does, why it's needed, and any non-obvious decision or edge case. Keep it to a few sentences — motivation first, then the key mechanics. When it matters, note the problem being solved, why this approach over the alternative, and what it affects downstream. State the *why*, not just the *what*. Reference real code (`file:line`) when it makes the explanation concrete. If something is genuinely ambiguous, say so. No quizzes, no checklists, no persona — just a clear, concise account of the work at each step.

## File & component size

- **Per-file line cap: 800.** Refactor before extending a file past **500**. Adjust both numbers to your language — these suit TypeScript and are too generous for some, too tight for others.
- One responsibility per file. Extract a unit when it starts doing two things you'd describe with an "and".
- Split by feature, not by file type.

## Stack defaults

Fill these in for your project — the agent will follow whatever you write, and guess if you leave it blank.

- **UI primitives:** (component library, or "hand-rolled")
- **Styling:** (approach, and what's banned)
- **State:** (default scope, and when to lift)
- **Routing:** (framework-native, or the router in use)
- **Data access:** (ORM/query builder/raw, and migration tool)

## LSP (read diagnostics before editing)

For any typed language with a language server, read the LSP diagnostics for the file before editing it. Don't ship code the LSP already flagged. This is a **pre-edit** check, not a post-edit check — catching a type error before you write the next line costs nothing; catching it after a 200-line refactor is expensive.

Typical servers: tsserver (TypeScript/JavaScript), pyright + ruff (Python), gopls (Go), rust-analyzer (Rust). Skip silently for untyped contexts (bash, plain markdown, config files).

A clean LSP at edit time prevents most of the "agent wrote code that doesn't compile" failure mode, and is far cheaper than a retro after a failed verify.

## Ordering invariants

Some pairs of actions have a required order. Getting it wrong doesn't always produce a loud error — sometimes the second step silently breaks because the first hasn't run. The harness encodes a few by default; your project will have its own.

**Encoded by the harness:**

- **PLAN → BUILD**: `/bencium-next` Phase B refuses to enter without explicit approval of Phase A. On Claude Code the `plan-gate` hook enforces this at the tool level, not just in the prompt.
- **RED → GREEN**: BUILD refuses to edit implementation files until the declared failing test is observed in red.
- **VERIFY (local) → DEPLOY**: `/bencium-deploy` refuses to run if `/bencium-verify` has any FAIL.
- **DEPLOY → SMOKE**: `[deployed]`-tagged ACCEPTANCE rows are never evaluated locally; only `/bencium-deploy` Step 5.5 may flip them.

**Pattern for your own:** if a step *must* run before another (database provisioned before secret request, auth before any user-route, migration before seed, lint before build), declare it in `.harness/rules.md` under an "Ordering invariants" heading. The agent reads `rules.md` every session and will refuse step Y without step X — the same enforcement model as the harness's own invariants.

Document the **why** on each one, not just the order. "Migration before seed (seed scripts assume the schema exists)" is enforceable; "migration before seed" alone forces the agent to guess the reason and may permit a clever-but-wrong shortcut.

## Cost discipline

- No inline base64 in source. Static assets go to object storage or a static directory.
- No copy-pasted blocks. If a block appears twice, extract on the second occurrence; three times, extract immediately.
- Name your columns — no `SELECT *`. No binary blobs in list or poll endpoints; return a `has_<field>` boolean and fetch the blob on demand.
- Paginate every list endpoint, with a default limit.
- LLM calls: start with the cheapest model that passes and escalate only when it fails; keep `max_tokens` tight; cache stable prompt prefixes.
- Annotate recurring-cost code with its expected cost, e.g. `// COST: ~$X/month at Y requests/day`.

## UI disclosure (always show what's happening)

The UI is the user's only window into the API, backend, and console. It must never hide work. Every async action must surface its state.

- **Loading**: any fetch/mutation/upload over ~150ms shows a visible indicator scoped to the affected element (button → spinner, list → skeleton, page → progress bar). No bare clicks that just "feel dead."
- **Progress**: long-running work (uploads, multi-step jobs, streaming responses) shows real progress — bytes uploaded, current step, tokens streamed. A static "Loading…" for over 3s is a bug.
- **Backend errors**: every failed request renders a user-visible message with at minimum the status or error code, and a retry or next-step affordance. No silent failures, no errors that appear only in the browser console.
- **Console as last resort**: nothing important goes only to `console.log`. Console is for debugging; UI is for users.
- **Disclosure for AI features**: model name, streaming state, token count when known, and stop/retry controls visible. Users must know when they're talking to a model, which one, and that it's still working.

Verified by the `UI discloses backend activity` row in `ACCEPTANCE.md`.

## TDD discipline (red → green)

- Every non-trivial task starts with a **failing test** that captures the "Done when" of the plan. Write the test, run it, observe red, *then* write the implementation.
- The plan in `/bencium-next` Phase A must name the test file and the assertion. Phase B refuses to edit implementation files until red is observed.
- Trivial tasks (typo, single-line rename, copy-only) may declare `Failing test: none — trivial (declared)`. Any other bypass requires an explicit `skip tdd` and a justification logged to `.harness/memory.md ## TDD bypasses`.
- A task is not done until the test passes locally **and** any related `[deployed]` ACCEPTANCE row passes against the live URL after `/bencium-deploy`.

## Browser & UAT testing

The harness ships **no** browser-test engine and imposes no runner. What it enforces is the *split*: local **TEST** (`✓`) proves behavior on your machine; deployed **SMOKE** (`🔥`) re-proves it against the live URL. A passing local browser test never flips a `[deployed]` row — only `/bencium-deploy` Step 5.5 can.

- **Local automated E2E** — whatever your stack or CI already uses. The harness only needs a command that exits non-zero on failure, wired into `.harness/config.yaml verify.cmd`, and a declared red→green test per plan.
- **Deployed UAT** — an interactive browser tool driving the live URL for screenshots, console errors, and network checks on `[deployed]` rows. Best for exploratory and visual acceptance.

Rule of thumb: automated E2E for deterministic CI gates, interactive browser checks for deployed UAT. Never let either stand in for the other — that's the whole point of the split.

## Quality gates

- No secrets in the repo. Environment variables plus `.gitignore`.
- Pagination on every list endpoint, with a default limit.
- **Verify pipelines end-to-end on both local and deployed environments.** Never trust local-only success for anything touching the network, environment variables, the build pipeline, or runtime — re-verify on the live URL via `/bencium-deploy` SMOKE before calling it shipped.

